          "id": "sec-1-6",
          "secNumber": "1.6",
          "heading": "Analytical Derivations and Assessment: Symmetrical Components and Sequence Network Modeling",
          "content": "# Unit 1 assessment: Power-system dynamics and rotor-angle stability\n\n**Submission rule:** Show dimensional steps, declare all bases and state which missing data prevent an operational conclusion. Problems use artificial scenarios.\n\n## Question group 1: Synchronous-machine electromechanics\n\n**Q1.1 \u2014 calculation.** For a positive mechanical minus electrical imbalance, predict the initial sign of angular acceleration. Explain whether a speed increase must instantly produce an equal change of electrical transfer. Present each quantity with units and report whether the answer is steady-state, transient, probability, energy or power, as appropriate.\n\n**Q1.2 \u2014 assumption stress test.** Choose a second value of inertia and recompute initial acceleration while leaving the same MVA base. Explain which term changes, then compare the result with the original simplified physical interpretation.\n\n**Q1.3 \u2014 applied critique.** Build a two-column list of measurable states and guessed parameters. From a simulated 100 ms angle record, estimate whether acceleration is changing. What additional voltage and mechanical input records would identify the cause?\n\n## Question group 2: Power-angle curves and synchronizing torque\n\n**Q2.1 \u2014 calculation.** Evaluate dPe/d\u03b4 at 30\u00b0, 60\u00b0 and 100\u00b0 and state where a single sine-curve equilibrium loses local positive restoring slope. Present each quantity with units and report whether the answer is steady-state, transient, probability, energy or power, as appropriate.\n\n**Q2.2 \u2014 assumption stress test.** Recompute Pmax if X rises by 20% with the same voltage magnitudes. Explain which term changes, then compare the result with the original simplified physical interpretation.\n\n**Q2.3 \u2014 applied critique.** At which delta does the first derivative become zero? Explain why setting a steady transfer exactly at that point leaves no positive synchronizing slope within the simplified model.\n\n## Question group 3: Transient stability and clearing intervals\n\n**Q3.1 \u2014 calculation.** Estimate the fault-on accelerating area between 30\u00b0 and 50\u00b0 under zero electrical power and constant Pm. Present each quantity with units and report whether the answer is steady-state, transient, probability, energy or power, as appropriate.\n\n**Q3.2 \u2014 assumption stress test.** Explain what topology and dynamic information is missing before a critical clearing time can be reported. Explain which term changes, then compare the result with the original simplified physical interpretation.\n\n**Q3.3 \u2014 applied critique.** Draw three separate transfer curves and label the machine angle at fault inception, fault clearance, and the limiting return point. Which quantities would a grid study need that this drawing omits?\n\n## Question group 4: Multimachine oscillations and damping\n\n**Q4.1 \u2014 calculation.** Convert a damped sinusoidal mode given in radians per second to hertz, then estimate envelope decay after one period. Present each quantity with units and report whether the answer is steady-state, transient, probability, energy or power, as appropriate.\n\n**Q4.2 \u2014 assumption stress test.** Explain why a stable local linear eigenmode does not rule out a large-disturbance loss of synchronism. Explain which term changes, then compare the result with the original simplified physical interpretation.\n\n**Q4.3 \u2014 applied critique.** Fit logarithmic peak amplitudes from an invented waveform, then report how your estimate changes when an early peak is removed. Explain why this sensitivity must be documented.\n\n## Final integrative question\n\nImagine the physical asset is operated under a new but plausible loading, controller or contingency condition. Identify two competing objectives, formulate a consistent indicator for each, and explain a trade-off. Provide a reproducible table with at least four rows and one sensitivity. Explicitly separate observed data from assumption-driven results.\n\n**Solution framework:** Select quantities that share the same physical boundary and time reference, prove unit consistency, locate binding constraints and state the domain of validity. A single \u201ccorrect\u201d design does not follow without a stated objective and validated site data.\n"
