          "id": "sec-2-4",
          "secNumber": "2.4",
          "heading": "Frequency nadir and reserve quality",
          "content": "# Unit 2, Lesson 4: Frequency nadir and reserve quality\n\n**Course position:** Frequency control and system resilience. **Study time:** approximately 90\u2013120 minutes including quantitative checks. **Model domain:** a synthetic, simplified calculation requiring interpretation. **Safety classification:** non-operational.\n\n## Physical idea and engineering context\n\nFrequency security depends not just on the total megawatts promised but also on response speed, energy duration, and the order in which resources are activated. The frequency nadir occurs when instantaneous net power imbalance becomes zero on the descent, not necessarily when aggregate reserve has fully deployed. Fast batteries can reduce nadir before a slow turbine responds; however, energy limits mean the response must eventually be handed over.\n\n## Governing model, boundaries and assumptions\n\n`Instantaneous power balance: 2H d(Delta f_pu)/dt = P_response(t) \u2212 P_disturbance \u2212 D Delta f_pu`\n\n## Worked quantitative example\n\nFor a 20 MW loss lasting until replacement, imagine Resource A supplies 15 MW after 0.2 s for 10 s and Resource B ramps from 0 to 20 MW over 10 s. At t=1 s, A plus B provide roughly 17 MW, leaving 3 MW shortfall before load effects. At t=8 s, the fast service has 15 MW and the slow one ~16 MW: the response exceeds the loss unless controls curtail one. Sum-of-capacities arithmetic is not a valid frequency trace without dispatch logic.\n\n## Interpretation and limiting cases\n\nA resource specifying \u201c20 MW reserve\u201d but no start time or duration has insufficient information for dynamic-security assessment. State the baseline dispatch and response measurement point.\n\n## What could make this answer wrong?\n\nModeling a battery as unlimited power and unlimited energy can produce a fictitious fast response. Frequency-protection thresholds must never be chosen from this toy calculation.\n\n## Applied comparison and design reasoning\n\nDesign a response stack with a 2-second fast service, a 30-second governor stage and a 5-minute replacement service. Identify the handover discontinuity if dispatch is not coordinated.\n\n## Guided problems\n\n**Problem A \u2014 first-principles check.** Show the minimum energy in MWh for a 15 MW service lasting 12 minutes without efficiency losses. Show the relationship used, a fully labeled unit conversion if needed, the computed result and an interpretive sentence.\n\n**Problem B \u2014 alternate assumption.** Explain how a 5-second delay can change nadir even if total deployed energy eventually matches. State which terms in the equation change and which remain fixed. If the problem lacks a unique numerical answer, identify the missing measurements rather than guessing.\n\n**Problem C \u2014 critical reading.** Why is an adequate total 10-minute energy budget insufficient to guarantee a safe early nadir? Write a short technical response containing one quantitative or logical check, one serious counterargument, and one criterion that would falsify your initial conclusion."
