          "content": "# Unit 2 project: Frequency event response with finite headroom\n\n## Question to answer\n\nHow much of an engineering conclusion is justified by the following small study, and what further data would be required to make a real operational or investment decision? Explain the difference between a physically interpretable calculation and a certified design or utility-approved action. All data below are synthetic, safe to explore in a spreadsheet or local script.\n\n## Case and fixed assumptions\n\nConsider a synthetic 500 MW-base island at 50 Hz with H=3.5 s. Simulate a \u221225 MW supply event at t=0. Use a first-order time-stepping toy equation: 2H df_pu/dt=DeltaP_pu+P_gov_pu\u2212D df_pu, D=1.0. Let P_gov ramp linearly from 0 to +20 MW over 6 s, and then saturate. Use time steps 0.02 s and 0.1 s. Contrast a 3 s-delayed ramp with immediate ramp. Report first 10 s frequency traces and the lowest simulated frequency for each assumption, explicitly labeling that it is not a protection threshold.\n\n## Deliverables and acceptance checks\n\nDeliver a table of discretization sensitivity, response headroom, nadir, and 10-second final deviation. Identify how fast reserves, load damping and under-frequency protection are omitted. Separate the static primary-response equation from the ramp transient.\n\n### Suggested grading rubric\n\n- 20%: physical model boundary and unit/sign correctness.\n- 25%: correct quantitative computation and explicit verification.\n- 20%: feasible state/limit handling over the full study interval.\n- 20%: meaningful sensitivity, comparison and uncertainty.\n- 15%: clear evidence-based conclusion and ethical/safety limitations."
