          "heading": "Analytical Derivations and Assessment: Symmetrical Three-Phase Faults and Circuit Breaker Ratings",
          "content": "# Unit 2 assessment: Frequency control and system resilience\n\n**Submission rule:** Show dimensional steps, declare all bases and state which missing data prevent an operational conclusion. Problems use artificial scenarios.\n\n## Question group 1: Frequency and the aggregate swing balance\n\n**Q1.1 \u2014 calculation.** For a \u22120.03 pu imbalance on a 50 Hz, H=3 s base, derive initial Hz/s with signs. Present each quantity with units and report whether the answer is steady-state, transient, probability, energy or power, as appropriate.\n\n**Q1.2 \u2014 assumption stress test.** Compare the same absolute MW event on a system with twice the relevant MW base. Explain which term changes, then compare the result with the original simplified physical interpretation.\n\n**Q1.3 \u2014 applied critique.** If H doubles but the absolute deficit stays fixed, which other base quantities must you hold constant before claiming that initial RoCoF halves?\n\n## Question group 2: Governor droop and primary response\n\n**Q2.1 \u2014 calculation.** If a theoretical droop command requires +10 MW but a governor has 6 MW headroom, label the result infeasible and calculate the unserved remainder. Present each quantity with units and report whether the answer is steady-state, transient, probability, energy or power, as appropriate.\n\n**Q2.2 \u2014 assumption stress test.** Describe one sensor trend distinguishing initial frequency decline from eventual static droop equilibrium. Explain which term changes, then compare the result with the original simplified physical interpretation.\n\n**Q2.3 \u2014 applied critique.** How does a saturated governor alter the effective droop slope seen by the system, and why is the final frequency not necessarily the one predicted by the original slopes?\n\n## Question group 3: Secondary control and area interchange\n\n**Q3.1 \u2014 calculation.** With actual tie flow 5 MW below schedule and bias term \u22124 MW, calculate ACE under the adopted sum convention. Present each quantity with units and report whether the answer is steady-state, transient, probability, energy or power, as appropriate.\n\n**Q3.2 \u2014 assumption stress test.** Describe why an incorrect tie-flow sign may trigger counterproductive correction. Explain which term changes, then compare the result with the original simplified physical interpretation.\n\n**Q3.3 \u2014 applied critique.** Construct a timeline of frequency, tie flow, ACE and dispatched control commands over 15 minutes and state at what point an alarm would need corroboration.\n\n## Question group 4: Frequency nadir and reserve quality\n\n**Q4.1 \u2014 calculation.** Show the minimum energy in MWh for a 15 MW service lasting 12 minutes without efficiency losses. Present each quantity with units and report whether the answer is steady-state, transient, probability, energy or power, as appropriate.\n\n**Q4.2 \u2014 assumption stress test.** Explain how a 5-second delay can change nadir even if total deployed energy eventually matches. Explain which term changes, then compare the result with the original simplified physical interpretation.\n\n**Q4.3 \u2014 applied critique.** Why is an adequate total 10-minute energy budget insufficient to guarantee a safe early nadir?\n\n## Final integrative question\n\nImagine the physical asset is operated under a new but plausible loading, controller or contingency condition. Identify two competing objectives, formulate a consistent indicator for each, and explain a trade-off. Provide a reproducible table with at least four rows and one sensitivity. Explicitly separate observed data from assumption-driven results.\n\n**Solution framework:** Select quantities that share the same physical boundary and time reference, prove unit consistency, locate binding constraints and state the domain of validity. A single \u201ccorrect\u201d design does not follow without a stated objective and validated site data.\n"
