          "content": "# Unit 4 project: HVDC transfer and terminal-cost screening\n\n## Question to answer\n\nHow much of an engineering conclusion is justified by the following small study, and what further data would be required to make a real operational or investment decision? Explain the difference between a physically interpretable calculation and a certified design or utility-approved action. All data below are synthetic, safe to explore in a spreadsheet or local script.\n\n## Case and fixed assumptions\n\nCompare two hypothetical 400 MW corridors over route lengths 100, 300 and 700 km without treating them as real projects. For the DC option assume V_DC=400 kV total defined voltage, R_loop=0.04 \u03a9/km and converter dissipation 5 MW at each end. At each distance calculate line loss and received MW at a constant 400 MW injected into the line, being explicit about whether station dissipation is upstream of this value. For a purely illustrative cost study use converter terminal capex $100M per terminal, DC line $0.75M/km, AC terminals $20M each and AC line $1.1M/km, excluding rights-of-way and reactive devices.\n\n## Deliverables and acceptance checks\n\nPlot illustrative total capital against distance and annotate why the intersection of toy straight lines is not a transferable HVDC break-even threshold. Add one scenario doubling converter capex and another requiring underground AC cable. Include an uncertainty register for capacity factor and reliability.\n\n### Suggested grading rubric\n\n- 20%: physical model boundary and unit/sign correctness.\n- 25%: correct quantitative computation and explicit verification.\n- 20%: feasible state/limit handling over the full study interval.\n- 20%: meaningful sensitivity, comparison and uncertainty.\n- 15%: clear evidence-based conclusion and ethical/safety limitations."
