          "content": "# Unit 4 assessment: High-voltage DC transmission and power electronics\n\n**Submission rule:** Show dimensional steps, declare all bases and state which missing data prevent an operational conclusion. Problems use artificial scenarios.\n\n## Question group 1: HVDC architectures and converters\n\n**Q1.1 \u2014 calculation.** Convert a \u00b1250 kV pole voltage to pole-to-pole voltage and calculate power at 800 A under a defined two-conductor path. Present each quantity with units and report whether the answer is steady-state, transient, probability, energy or power, as appropriate.\n\n**Q1.2 \u2014 assumption stress test.** Point out the AC and DC measurement locations where converter losses may appear. Explain which term changes, then compare the result with the original simplified physical interpretation.\n\n**Q1.3 \u2014 applied critique.** A diagram says \u201c\u00b1320 kV\u201d and an export table says \u201c640 kV.\u201d Show how both statements can be consistent and what current must accompany the power entry.\n\n## Question group 2: DC-line losses and operating limits\n\n**Q2.1 \u2014 calculation.** If current decreases by 20% with unchanged R, determine the new I\u00b2R loss fraction. Present each quantity with units and report whether the answer is steady-state, transient, probability, energy or power, as appropriate.\n\n**Q2.2 \u2014 assumption stress test.** Explain why higher voltage is not free from insulation and terminal-cost consequences. Explain which term changes, then compare the result with the original simplified physical interpretation.\n\n**Q2.3 \u2014 applied critique.** Derive the percentage of sent energy lost over a 10-hour constant operation with the 400 kV case, and state why using this result for an annual business case is unreliable.\n\n## Question group 3: VSC active/reactive control and grid support\n\n**Q3.1 \u2014 calculation.** At Smax=50 MVA and P=30 MW, calculate ideal Q capability at fixed interface voltage. Present each quantity with units and report whether the answer is steady-state, transient, probability, energy or power, as appropriate.\n\n**Q3.2 \u2014 assumption stress test.** Describe one fault-voltage condition under which this steady circle ceases to be sufficient. Explain which term changes, then compare the result with the original simplified physical interpretation.\n\n**Q3.3 \u2014 applied critique.** Compute the necessary derating of active power to provide 80 MVAr within the simplified 100 MVA circle.\n\n## Question group 4: Multiterminal DC and DC fault challenges\n\n**Q4.1 \u2014 calculation.** Determine stored energy of 2 mF at 10 kV and at 20 kV. Present each quantity with units and report whether the answer is steady-state, transient, probability, energy or power, as appropriate.\n\n**Q4.2 \u2014 assumption stress test.** Explain why equal capacitor energy does not imply equal DC fault current. Explain which term changes, then compare the result with the original simplified physical interpretation.\n\n**Q4.3 \u2014 applied critique.** Why can two DC networks with identical voltage and capacitance experience different peak fault currents? Name at least three missing physical parameters.\n\n## Final integrative question\n\nImagine the physical asset is operated under a new but plausible loading, controller or contingency condition. Identify two competing objectives, formulate a consistent indicator for each, and explain a trade-off. Provide a reproducible table with at least four rows and one sensitivity. Explicitly separate observed data from assumption-driven results.\n\n**Solution framework:** Select quantities that share the same physical boundary and time reference, prove unit consistency, locate binding constraints and state the domain of validity. A single \u201ccorrect\u201d design does not follow without a stated objective and validated site data.\n"
