          "content": "# Unit 7 project: Microgrid island adequacy by hour\n\n## Question to answer\n\nHow much of an engineering conclusion is justified by the following small study, and what further data would be required to make a real operational or investment decision? Explain the difference between a physically interpretable calculation and a certified design or utility-approved action. All data below are synthetic, safe to explore in a spreadsheet or local script.\n\n## Case and fixed assumptions\n\nCreate a notional critical-load profile [1.2,1.4,1.6,1.3] MW, solar profile [0.5,0.3,0.0,0.0] MW, and a generator able to deliver 1.0 MW continuously. Battery initial usable energy above minimum is 2 MWh, max discharge power is 0.6 MW and discharge efficiency 0.9. Use four 1-hour steps to calculate hourly power gaps, battery feasible discharge, unmet critical load, and the next energy state. You may curtail surplus energy but should not charge the battery without explicitly including charging efficiency.\n\n## Deliverables and acceptance checks\n\nReport any uncovered MW and MWh, state-of-charge constraint violations and a revised critical-load schedule that reduces unserved energy. Do not assert that the physical island is safe; include the missing grounding, relay and transfer-control studies.\n\n### Suggested grading rubric\n\n- 20%: physical model boundary and unit/sign correctness.\n- 25%: correct quantitative computation and explicit verification.\n- 20%: feasible state/limit handling over the full study interval.\n- 20%: meaningful sensitivity, comparison and uncertainty.\n- 15%: clear evidence-based conclusion and ethical/safety limitations."
