          "content": "# Unit 7 assessment: Distribution automation and microgrid operation\n\n**Submission rule:** Show dimensional steps, declare all bases and state which missing data prevent an operational conclusion. Problems use artificial scenarios.\n\n## Question group 1: Radial feeder modeling and voltage profiles\n\n**Q1.1 \u2014 calculation.** Combine two approximate per-unit drops 0.02 and 0.035 with a nominal source of 1.01 pu. Present each quantity with units and report whether the answer is steady-state, transient, probability, energy or power, as appropriate.\n\n**Q1.2 \u2014 assumption stress test.** Explain why unequal phases require a richer model than a single series branch. Explain which term changes, then compare the result with the original simplified physical interpretation.\n\n**Q1.3 \u2014 applied critique.** Draw nodal power balances at the remote branch and specify how a phase-unbalance study would change the scalar model.\n\n## Question group 2: Volt-VAR coordination and tap changers\n\n**Q2.1 \u2014 calculation.** Calculate net voltage change if one control raises 0.012 pu and another reduces 0.007 pu in a locally linear model. Present each quantity with units and report whether the answer is steady-state, transient, probability, energy or power, as appropriate.\n\n**Q2.2 \u2014 assumption stress test.** State one measure of excessive tap activity in a control study. Explain which term changes, then compare the result with the original simplified physical interpretation.\n\n**Q2.3 \u2014 applied critique.** What event log and supervisory data are needed to prove a reduction in tap-change count rather than merely a better voltage snapshot?\n\n## Question group 3: Microgrid transition and island energy balance\n\n**Q3.1 \u2014 calculation.** Given 1.4 MW load, 0.4 MW PV, 0.7 MW dispatchable and 0.2 MW battery output, find residual deficit. Present each quantity with units and report whether the answer is steady-state, transient, probability, energy or power, as appropriate.\n\n**Q3.2 \u2014 assumption stress test.** Calculate one-hour deficit energy if it remains constant and identify which load-priority information is missing. Explain which term changes, then compare the result with the original simplified physical interpretation.\n\n**Q3.3 \u2014 applied critique.** List the information needed to determine which noncritical loads can be deferred under a cloudy three-hour outage; distinguish engineering needs from permissions and life-safety rules.\n\n## Question group 4: Reliability indices and feeder restoration\n\n**Q4.1 \u2014 calculation.** For 500 customers, one event affects 100 for 2 h and another 200 for 0.5 h. Calculate SAIFI and SAIDI. Present each quantity with units and report whether the answer is steady-state, transient, probability, energy or power, as appropriate.\n\n**Q4.2 \u2014 assumption stress test.** Explain which highly affected customers might be hidden by the averages. Explain which term changes, then compare the result with the original simplified physical interpretation.\n\n**Q4.3 \u2014 applied critique.** Construct an event table with start, restoration, affected customers and event category, then independently recompute the indices and one high-quantile customer impact.\n\n## Final integrative question\n\nImagine the physical asset is operated under a new but plausible loading, controller or contingency condition. Identify two competing objectives, formulate a consistent indicator for each, and explain a trade-off. Provide a reproducible table with at least four rows and one sensitivity. Explicitly separate observed data from assumption-driven results.\n\n**Solution framework:** Select quantities that share the same physical boundary and time reference, prove unit consistency, locate binding constraints and state the domain of validity. A single \u201ccorrect\u201d design does not follow without a stated objective and validated site data.\n"
